n, effect/side-effect class, caller grant intersected with the tool's own contract, timeout/retry/error mapping and required review/secret/taint policy. Emit actual invocation and policy/review decision records with replay identity; reject ungranted or out-of-contract effects, missing required review, invalid/tainted sink values, secret-policy violations and live calls in recorded-output segments. Prompt text, retrieved data and review records cannot create capability authority; preserve explicitly permitted alternatives for audit-only/interactive contexts. The semantic producer retains its compilation, policy and provider responsibilities. This library leaf must expose the same accepted/refused predicate through its own callable boundary, retaining actual call/results, typed failures, schema/effect/grant/replay/origin identities as applicable. Reject a bypassed or weakened check, a mismatched provider result, or an export/manifest name used instead of actual API invocation. This is consumer acceptance, not new evidence that the underlying service or API is implemented. Library consumer criteria interface/behavior/a1/human-review-gate, interface/behavior/a1/tool-authority: Invoke the actual STD13 public API against declared Domain/Runtime providers under this retained semantic contract: Source acceptance for A1-A11 remains open. A1: lower all declared AI forms through syntax, typed core and legal profiles into linked manifests, preserving source/macro origins, every AI effect, explicit replay modes and runtime audit records. Reject ambient credentials, hidden adapter effects, missing model or schema identities, generated source bypassing the normal compiler pipeline, and privileged deployments without review policy or required evaluation evidence. A4-A5: emit tool and agent manifests with complete dependencies and separate memory read/write modes. Tool declaration, agent toolset, package/deployment grant, scoped runtime handle and policy must all agree. Plans and mo